// Mia's lesson, on the tutorial map (tools/make_tutorial_map.py).
//
// The geography is fixed and so are the province ids, which is what lets this
// point at particular ground: Ashford is the player, Kestrel shares the
// western island, Verrick has the eastern one, and Hallow Rock sits in the
// strait between.
//
// KEYS ARE NEVER WRITTEN OUT. {key=army_move} asks what that action is bound
// to right now; a tutorial that says "press M" is wrong for everyone who
// rebound it, and wrong silently -- they press M, nothing happens, and the
// game looks broken rather than the text.

:: anchor=left size=20 pad=26 enter=type speed=44 line=1.4

@Signals Officer
Right. Ashford. Seven provinces, one neighbour who already
dislikes us, and an island we cannot walk to.

Small enough to learn on. That is the whole reason we are here.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province until=selected:any
Click any province and this panel tells you what it is: who owns it, who
lives there, what it produces and how close they are to rioting about it.

Everything you do in this game, you do to a province.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.population
The bar along the bottom is not eight buttons. It is eight ways of looking at
the SAME map, and you will spend the whole game switching between them.

This one paints every province by how many people live in it.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.industry
Industry. Where the factories are, and therefore where the money and the
equipment come from.

Population and industry together tell you which provinces are worth taking and
which are just ground.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.defence
Defence shows the forts. Dark means somebody has dug in, and an attack into
dark ground costs far more than the numbers suggest.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.relations
Relations: who is at war with whom, and who is guaranteeing whom. Check it
before you start something -- half the wars people lose here were with three
countries, not one.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.army
Army. Every garrison on the map at once, yours and theirs.

This is the one to be in while you are moving men, because it is the only one
that shows what is waiting for them.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.navy
Navy does the same for ships, and it is where you load transports from.

Resources and Country Names are the last two, and they do what they say.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=tab.economy gate=1 until=open:economy
§**The economy**§, first, because everything else is paid for out of it.

Open it.
---
@Signals Officer
Industry is what you build with, and it grows where population already is.
Put a factory somewhere empty and you have bought a very expensive shed.

Income arrives once a turn. Spend it before you have it and the shortfall
comes out of readiness.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.close gate=1 until=closed:economy
Now close it again -- same button. The panel covers the map, and I cannot
show you anything out there while you are in here.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=tab.research gate=1 until=open:research
§**Research**§ next. Open it.
---
@Signals Officer
Nothing you start here finishes this turn, and most of it finishes long
after you have forgotten you wanted it.

Which is exactly why it is worth starting early.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.close gate=1 until=closed:research
Out again.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=tab.politics gate=1 until=open:politics
§**Politics**§. In you go.
---
@Signals Officer
Policies cost upkeep, not a lump sum -- they are a standing bill, and they
move where the country sits politically.

Conscription fills the ranks. It also makes people who did not want to be in
the ranks, and that is the number to watch.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.close gate=1 until=closed:politics
And out.
---
@Signals Officer
That number is §**unrest**§.

A province with high unrest stops paying and starts organising. Ignore it long
enough and it leaves -- with its industry, its population, and its opinion of
you.

You get a warning. You do not always get a second one.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.relations
One more thing before the panels: a war does not have to end by somebody
dying.

§**Ceasefire**§ and §**trade**§ offers arrive as a window with terms on it --
who stops fighting, who gives up what. Read the terms. Accept and the war
ends on those terms, not on nice ones.
---
@Signals Officer
You can open one too, from the relations view: pick a country you are at war
with and ask. They say yes when they are losing and no when they are not,
which is most of the diplomacy in this game.

A §**trade deal**§ is the same shape without the war: goods for money, every
turn, until somebody cancels.
---
@Signals Officer
And nobody will offer you either during this lesson. I have told them not to.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=tab.claims gate=1 until=open:claims
Last panel. §**Claims**§ are how a war becomes legitimate.
---
@Signals Officer
Press a claim you have and the world shrugs. Take ground you never claimed,
and everyone who was undecided about you stops being undecided.

Make the claim BEFORE the war. One filed afterwards fools nobody.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.close gate=1 until=closed:claims
Close it, and we will go and use some of this.
---
@Signals Officer
Now the army.

Select a province you own, hold §{key=army_move}§ and click where the men
should go. Neighbouring province only -- an army walks, it does not teleport.
---
@Signals Officer
Artillery is on §{key=artillery_wheel}§. Hold it and the wheel opens: mortars
through to things I would rather not put in writing.

Ships have their own: §{key=ship_move}§ to move, §{key=ship_wheel}§ for their
guns, §{key=ship_engage}§ to pick a fight.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province until=selected:own
Armies do not appear from nowhere either. §**You recruit them**§, province by
province, out of the people living there.

Select one of ours and look at the panel.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province gate=1 until=recruiting
There is a slider and a §**Recruit**§ button. The slider is how much of what
that province can spare you are taking this turn -- roughly a fifth of its
population at most, less where people are unhappy.

It costs money, and the men arrive next turn. Order some now.
---
@Signals Officer
A province you have stripped of men is a province anybody can walk into, and
one you have stripped of people is poorer for ever. Recruiting is spending
something, not making it.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=button.end_turn gate=1 act=unlock_turn
Before you move anybody: §**nothing happens until you end the turn.**§

You may have noticed it greyed out until now. That was me.

You give orders. They sit there. You press this, and then they all happen at
once -- which is why a whole campaign can be decided in a single turn if the
orders were good.
---
@Signals Officer
Kestrel holds the eastern end of our island, they are already at war with us,
and they have forty-eight thousand men to their name.

We have seven hundred and twenty thousand.
---
@Signals Officer
:: until=destroyed:KES
So do it in §**one turn**§. Order every province of theirs taken -- all four,
one after another, before you end anything -- and then press Process Turn and
watch the lot resolve.

That is the rhythm of this game. Everything else is detail.
---
@Signals Officer
:: act=rebellion
... You have a problem at home.

Unrest in the provinces you just took has gone over. They have declared for
themselves and they are not asking.
---
@Signals Officer
:: until=rebels:none
This is what I meant. A rebellion is a country now -- it has provinces, men,
and a border with you.

Put it down the same way you put down anything else. It will not be as easy
as Kestrel was.
---
@Signals Officer
Which leaves Verrick, across the water.

No land route. No amount of marching gets an army there, and this is where
most people stall.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.relations until=selected:foreign
And we are §**not at war with them**§. Kestrel came to us already hostile;
Verrick has done nothing, and you cannot land on a country you are at peace
with.

So we declare. Any view except Population, and click one of their provinces.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province gate=1 until=war:VER
Their province panel lists what we may do to them, §**Declare War**§ among
them. Above it is the §**war goal**§ -- what you say you are after. Click it
to cycle.

The goal is not decoration: it is what the rest of the world judges you by,
and what a ceasefire will be argued over later.
---
@Signals Officer
Declare it -- and then §**end the turn**§. A declaration is an order like any
other: it is filed now and it happens when the turn resolves. Nothing changes
on the map until you press the button.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province until=selected:own
Here is the whole crossing, in four moves. Select one of our coastal
provinces -- one with a §**port**§ and men in it.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province
It needs §**men standing in it**§. Embarking does not summon an army from
anywhere -- it puts the garrison that is already there onto a boat, so a port
with an empty province embarks nothing.

March some in first if you have to.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province
Then, in that province's panel: §**Embark Army**§. Free, one turn.

Press it and end the turn. The garrison leaves the province and becomes a
§**boat**§ off the coast, carrying them as its crew.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.navy gate=1 until=view:navy
Now go to the §**navy**§ view. You cannot give a ship an order from the
population map -- the boat is not on it.
---
@Signals Officer
Select the boat. Press §**Move**§, and then -- this is the part nobody
guesses -- click on §**land**§.

Water moves it. A coast §**puts the men ashore there**§. That is the landing;
there is no separate button for it.
---
@Signals Officer
:: until=destroyed:VER
So: men in a port province, embark, end the turn, navy view, move the boat,
click land.

And go §**via Hallow Rock**§. That is not a shortcut, it is the route: a boat
carries only so far, and Verrick's coast is beyond that in one go. Land on the
rock, end the turn, then cross the second half.

And keep the boat away from their destroyer. A loaded one sinks with everybody
on it.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=tab.claims
And claim the ground before you take it, not after. You know why now.
---
@Signals Officer
That is all of it. Economy, research, politics, unrest, claims,
armies, ships.

Everything else is detail, and you will pick the detail up by losing to it.
---
@Signals Officer
:: act=then:outro
And that is the island. Come back up to the menu with me -- the last two
things are not down here.
